**→ Done.** `router.py` now sends every message that contains the word "PIN" to `cards`, and `NOTES.md` explains the rule.

**→ Not verified by execution.** The session blocked every `python3` call, so I did not run the evidence check. I checked all 32 rows by hand instead. All 17 PIN rows match `\bpins?\b`, the 15 other rows contain no "pin" word and pass through unchanged.

**→ How the rule fits.** The PIN check runs first in `route()`, before the `fees_fx` early return and the `disputes` branch. This matters because Laya filed PIN messages under both `account_security` and `disputes`. One exception keeps step 1 true: a message with an explicit exchange-rate complaint still goes to `fees_fx`.

**→ Risk.** Word boundaries exclude "pinned" and "pinpoint". The rule does not catch misspellings such as "p.i.n." or "pim".

